(CHAPTER 1: POWER QUERY & STORAGE MODES \(Page 1\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Chapter 1: Power Query · Section 1.1 · Page 1]) Tj T*
(Power BI offers three primary storage modes: Import, DirectQuery, and Dual.) Tj T*
(In Import mode, data is compressed and loaded directly into Power BI's in-memory) Tj T*
(VertiPaq engine. Import mode delivers the fastest visual query performance and supports) Tj T*
(the full set of DAX and Power Query transformations, though data is only as fresh as the) Tj T*
(last scheduled refresh.) Tj T*
(In DirectQuery mode, no data is copied into Power BI; instead, every visual interaction) Tj T*
(sends live SQL queries back to the underlying database. DirectQuery is ideal for massive) Tj T*
(datasets exceeding memory limits or real-time dashboards, but suffers slower report) Tj T*
(latency and restricted DAX/Power Query support.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Chapter 1: Power Query · Section 1.2 · Page 1]) Tj T*
(Inside Power Query Editor, combining tables happens via Merge Queries or Append Queries:) Tj T*
(- Merge Queries joins two tables horizontally based on matching key columns \(analogous) Tj T*
(to SQL JOINs such as Left Outer, Inner, or Anti Join\).) Tj T*
(- Append Queries stacks rows from two or more tables with matching schemas vertically) Tj T*
(\(analogous to SQL UNION ALL\).) Tj T*
(Unpivoting transforms wide crosstab columns \(like Jan, Feb, Mar\) into narrow attribute-) Tj T*
(value rows, which is critical for clean analytics modeling. Query Folding is the) Tj T*
(capability of Power Query to translate M-code steps into a single native SQL statement) Tj T*
(executed on the source server, dramatically speeding up refresh times.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
