(CHAPTER 2: STAR SCHEMA & DATA MODELING \(Page 2\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Chapter 2: Data Modeling · Section 2.1 · Page 2]) Tj T*
(The gold standard for Power BI data modeling is the Star Schema. A Star Schema separates) Tj T*
(data into:) Tj T*
(1. Fact Tables: Tall, narrow tables storing quantitative numeric observations \(e.g.,) Tj T*
(SalesAmount, Quantity\) and foreign keys, typically updated continuously.) Tj T*
(2. Dimension Tables: Short, wide tables storing descriptive business attributes used for) Tj T*
(filtering and grouping \(e.g., DimProduct, DimCustomer, DimDate\).) Tj T*
(Keeping a Star Schema rather than a flat single table or deep Snowflake Schema reduces) Tj T*
(model size in VertiPaq, simplifies DAX formulas, and eliminates ambiguous filter paths.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Chapter 2: Data Modeling · Section 2.2 · Page 2]) Tj T*
(Relationships connect Dimension tables \(the '1' side\) to Fact tables \(the '*' many side\)) Tj T*
(in a One-to-Many \(1:*\) relationship. By default, Cross-Filter Direction should be set to) Tj T*
(Single, meaning filters flow from Dimension tables down to Fact tables. Bi-directional) Tj T*
(\(Both\) cross-filtering should be avoided unless strictly necessary because it introduces) Tj T*
(circular dependency risks, ambiguity, and significant performance overhead.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Chapter 2: Data Modeling · Section 2.3 · Page 2]) Tj T*
(Dedicated Date Dimension tables are mandatory for reliable Time Intelligence. A proper) Tj T*
(Date table must contain contiguous dates without gaps covering full years from start to) Tj T*
(end, have a unique Date column marked as 'Mark as Date Table', and avoid relying on) Tj T*
(Power BI's hidden Auto Date/Time tables which bloat model memory.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
